# Didache Chapter 8 — The LOGOS Bible (Formal Equivalent)

**Translator:** LOGOS | **Source:** Codex Hierosolymitanus (H), Didache 8 | **Source Image:** source-texts/greek-EC/DID_008_page_001.jpg

---

## Chapter 8

**1** Do not observe your fasts at the same time as the hypocrites — for they fast on the second and fifth days of the week. Instead, fast on the fourth day and on the Day of Preparation.

**2** Do not pray as the hypocrites do. Rather, pray as the Lord commanded in his Gospel:

> Our Father, who art in heaven,
> hallowed be thy name;
> thy kingdom come;
> thy will be done,
> as in heaven, so also on earth;
> give us today our daily bread;
> and forgive us our debt,
> as we also forgive our debtors;
> and lead us not into temptation,
> but deliver us from evil;
> for thine is the power and the glory forever.

**3** Pray this way three times each day.

---

*LOGOS notes: V.1 — "Second and fifth days" = Monday and Thursday. The Didache community differentiated itself by fasting Wednesday ("fourth day") and Friday ("Day of Preparation" = παρασκευή, the day before the Sabbath). The Wednesday fast was likely associated with the betrayal (Judas's agreement with the priests, Matt 26:14-16, traditionally on a Wednesday); Friday with the crucifixion. V.2 — "his Gospel" (τὸ εὐαγγέλιον αὐτοῦ) — the earliest reference to a written Gospel as a normative text? Or to the oral preaching of the Lord? This is debated. The Lord's Prayer here is close to Matt 6:9-13 but with the doxology ("power and glory forever") absent from Matthew's best manuscripts. The Didache preserves a liturgical form that was already in use by the late 1st century.*
